WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:11.030
Here’s a challenge for you: After you finish
watching this video, go to your kitchen and

00:00:11.030 --> 00:00:12.030
open the fridge.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:15.960
Then, try to find something that has been
produced outside of your country.

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.670
And if you’re watching this video from a
European Country, try to find some product

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:22.200
produced outside of the European Union.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:26.940
I bet you can’t even find one.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:30.739
Doesn’t it surprise you?

00:00:30.739 --> 00:00:36.420
Well, think about it… your Mobile phone
has been manufactured in more than 20 different

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:37.420
countries.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:41.969
The pants you’re wearing right were manufactured
in a country like China or Bangladesh.

00:00:41.969 --> 00:00:45.460
And maybe the company you work for is also
foreign.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:46.630
No surprises here, right?

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:50.100
We all agree that we live in a globalized
economy.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:57.620
Nonetheless, food is the exception to the
rule.

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:02.249
Agriculture is an industry that is completely
protected from foreign competition.

00:01:02.249 --> 00:01:05.909
And make no mistake: this has nothing to do
with Donald Trump.

00:01:05.909 --> 00:01:08.780
In fact, if you think he’s the creator of
trade wars…

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:11.240
Well, you’re absolutely wrong.

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:15.500
Actually, the primary sector exists in a never
ending cold war.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:21.369
That’s why, from time to time, we see headlines
like this one…

00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:26.909
Trade War Rips Through Farm World; Soybean,
Hog Prices Fall

00:01:26.909 --> 00:01:29.070
Or this other one…

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:32.520
EU cuts import tariffs in a bid to end 'banana
wars'

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.050
But there are more than tariffs here.

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:39.220
In fact, tariffs are just one of the many
tools used to attack foreign markets.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:41.049
And it’s not even the deadliest.

00:01:41.049 --> 00:01:46.049
Here’s an example: America spends more than
20 billion dollars a year in subsidies for

00:01:46.049 --> 00:01:47.049
their farmers.

00:01:47.049 --> 00:01:48.049
Sound like a lot?

00:01:48.049 --> 00:01:53.659
Well, not compared to the whopping 72 billion
dollars that the EU spends in subsidies every

00:01:53.659 --> 00:01:54.659
year.

00:01:54.659 --> 00:01:59.540
And now you might wonder… but what do subsidies
have to do with trade wars?

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:02.509
The answer is…

00:02:02.509 --> 00:02:04.490
A LOT!

00:02:04.490 --> 00:02:09.950
For example, this milk break you see on the
screen is worth an average of 60 euro cents

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:11.960
in a Spanish supermarket.

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:14.910
From those 60 cents, the Spanish farmer would
get 30 cents.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:19.310
But his actual revenue is going to be 44 cents.

00:02:19.310 --> 00:02:20.310
Why?

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:28.030
Because the European Union will give him a
subsidy of 14 cents per liter.

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:31.420
Thanks to this Grant, the farmer can sell
cheap and still make Money.

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:36.220
And this is why Europe doesn’t need to buy
African sugar despite it being cheaper.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:40.890
Or even more surprisingly, why American or
European producers can smash the competition

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:44.630
in African markets despite having higher wages.

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:48.580
But wait a minute because things are about
to change.

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:53.870
And this time, the free market champion is
not America, nor Japan, nor Australia…

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:59.640
My dear viewers, despite what you might expect,
if there is somebody willing to start an agricultural

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:06.170
liberalization, it’s the EUROPEAN UNION.

00:03:06.170 --> 00:03:09.920
Emmanuel Macron breaks French taboo on farm
subsidies

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:14.900
A French President questioning the subsidies
on farming is like… a hipster closing his

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:16.780
instagram account.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:18.170
Something never seen before.

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:23.170
For years, the so called, “Common Agricultural
Policy”, also known as the CAP has been

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:25.650
one of the pillars of the European Union.

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:28.890
And France is, by far, its biggest supporter.

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:32.541
But, as I said, things are about to change.

00:03:32.541 --> 00:03:33.660
So now the question is…

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:38.060
Is Europe ready to liberalize its agricultural
sector?

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:41.810
Are we about to see the end of the food trade
wars?

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:45.900
And most importantly of all… is this good
or bad news?

00:03:45.900 --> 00:03:51.010
In this video, we are going to answer all
of these questions but, before we do, let’s

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:54.200
take a look back at history.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:58.700
WHAT IS CAP USED FOR?

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:02.690
Let’s imagine we have a wheat farm in ESSEX,
in the United Kingdom.

00:04:02.690 --> 00:04:10.300
We harvest the crop and go to sell it to the
market.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:14.280
Last time we did, we received 175 Pounds per
ton.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.949
So it makes sense to expect to sell it for
a similar price this time around, right?

00:04:18.949 --> 00:04:19.959
Well… no.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:26.000
This time, maybe the market price for wheat
is just 150 pounds per ton.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.580
This is the reality every farmer faces every
year.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:35.259
By contrast, the price of an iPhone doesn’t
change much over time, so if we sell iPhones,

00:04:35.259 --> 00:04:37.629
we can make good predictions about our income.

00:04:37.629 --> 00:04:42.819
But wheat, corn, oil or any other commodity
can change their market price from one day

00:04:42.819 --> 00:04:47.229
to the other.

00:04:47.229 --> 00:04:49.319
And now you might wonder…

00:04:49.319 --> 00:04:50.319
why?

00:04:50.319 --> 00:04:52.860
What’s the difference between wheat and
an iPhone?

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:56.599
Well, the difference is that commodities are
the same everywhere.

00:04:56.599 --> 00:04:58.410
Did you eat bread today?

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:02.070
Could you tell me where the wheat it was made
from was produced?

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:03.600
Almost certainly not.

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:07.680
The same goes with the oil used to produce
the gasoline you put on your car.

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:12.349
You don’t know if it is Saudi or Canadian
oil and… you don’t care.

00:05:12.349 --> 00:05:17.219
You just want it to be as cheap as possible.

00:05:17.219 --> 00:05:23.129
Nonetheless, there are just a few dinosaur
juice producers…

00:05:23.129 --> 00:05:26.889
while wheat, corn and potatoes can be harvested
anywhere.

00:05:26.889 --> 00:05:31.620
So, if one big producer had a good harvest…
that’s going to affect the market price

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:33.770
for everybody.

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:34.810
Add to this the weather.

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:39.849
Too little rain, too much rain, or an unusually
cold winter can be enough to kill off all

00:05:39.849 --> 00:05:40.900
of your revenue.

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:46.009
Nonetheless, we need agriculture because,
unfortunately, you cannot eat dinosaur juice

00:05:46.009 --> 00:05:50.699
or iPhones.

00:05:50.699 --> 00:05:54.280
So how can we keep eating while farmers don’t
starve?

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:59.030
This might seem like a stupid question, but
believe me, it is one of the hardest problems

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:00.030
in economics.

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:04.520
And this is where the Common Agricultural
Policy, also known as the CAP, comes into

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:05.520
play.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:10.229
This is how the European Union protects their
farmers.

00:06:10.229 --> 00:06:17.279
When we talk about CAP, we’re talking about
the cornerstone of the European Union.

00:06:17.279 --> 00:06:21.189
It was a thing even before the Schenghen area
or the Eurozone where born.

00:06:21.189 --> 00:06:22.189
Actually…

00:06:22.189 --> 00:06:24.550
do you know who was the actual father of the
CAP?

00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:25.550
Exactly!

00:06:25.550 --> 00:06:30.210
The former French president, Charles de Gaulle…

00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:35.250
“A nation has no friends.

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:36.389
Only interests.”

00:06:36.389 --> 00:06:38.249
–Charles De Gaulle.

00:06:38.249 --> 00:06:41.889
If you’ve been subscribed to VisualPolitik
for a long time, you already know that Charles

00:06:41.889 --> 00:06:45.850
de Gaulle was not a big fan of the free market
economy.

00:06:45.850 --> 00:06:49.539
Well… he wasn’t a big fan of anything
except himself, really.

00:06:49.539 --> 00:06:53.419
This is why, when France became a member of
the European Single Market, what later would

00:06:53.419 --> 00:06:56.610
become the European Union, he added a condition.

00:06:56.610 --> 00:07:02.229
“OK, we’ll sign up here buuuut, you gotta
promise us that nobody is gonna become competition

00:07:02.229 --> 00:07:03.419
for our farmers”.

00:07:03.419 --> 00:07:09.110
And this is how the CAP was born: a policy
that is so interventionist, it makes Bernie

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:13.080
Sanders look like Warren Buffet.

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:19.991
Basically, in order to compensate for potential
loses when market prices go down, the European

00:07:19.991 --> 00:07:22.319
Union gives them direct cash.

00:07:22.319 --> 00:07:27.710
For example, if you have a barn with cows
in Spain, Brussels will give you cash straight

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:30.229
away for each cow you own.

00:07:30.229 --> 00:07:34.099
And if you wanna buy a milking machine that
is going to make you more productive…

00:07:34.099 --> 00:07:36.240
Brussels will help you too.

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:38.300
This way Europe is not guaranteeing your income.

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:43.349
It is also helping protect you from American
or African producers.

00:07:43.349 --> 00:07:48.900
And as you might have already imagined, all
this aid costs a shitload of money (read it

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:51.509
as it is so I can censor it).

00:07:51.509 --> 00:07:58.279
If you wanna get a better idea, during the
90s, this CAP comprised 80% of the whole European

00:07:58.279 --> 00:07:59.279
Budget.

00:07:59.279 --> 00:08:06.389
And now that Europe is bigger and has more
things to do, CAP still makes up 40% of all

00:08:06.389 --> 00:08:07.910
EU spending.

00:08:07.910 --> 00:08:09.310
And… surprise surprise!

00:08:09.310 --> 00:08:11.819
Guess which country receives the most aid!

00:08:11.819 --> 00:08:13.059
Of course, that would be…

00:08:13.059 --> 00:08:14.059
FRANCE!

00:08:14.059 --> 00:08:17.400
But wait a minute because things don’t stop
there.

00:08:17.400 --> 00:08:29.419
Beyond direct aid, Europe has other tactics
to avoid price fluctuation: quotas and tariffs.

00:08:29.419 --> 00:08:34.070
For example, in order to avoid a price drop
in milk, Europe used to but a production limit

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:35.279
on each country.

00:08:35.279 --> 00:08:38.810
That means that you couldn’t produce more
than a certain number of liters.

00:08:38.810 --> 00:08:43.150
And, of course… who was benefiting the most
from those production quotas?

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:47.150
You can guess… the country of croissants
and baguettes.

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:52.870
As you can imagine, this milk quota created
all kinds of problems among the other members.

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:57.061
And this is why, despite French pressure,
Europe had to put and end to this.

00:08:57.061 --> 00:09:05.460
END OF 
EUROPEAN MILK QUOTAS: A NEW ERA FOR THE FRENCH

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:06.460
DAIRY SECTOR

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:09.020
And, of course, we also have the tariffs.

00:09:09.020 --> 00:09:15.190
Tariffs are like sniper bullets to stop very
specific products from getting into European

00:09:15.190 --> 00:09:16.190
markets.

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:19.960
For example, imagine that you are an Argentinan
coffee producer.

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:25.310
If you want to sell your RAW coffee, meaning
you take it from the plant and sell it, Europe

00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:26.910
has no problem.

00:09:26.910 --> 00:09:29.030
0 tariffs for you.

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:30.180
Why?

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:32.560
Because no European country grows coffee.

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:33.560
But, oh boy!

00:09:33.560 --> 00:09:37.060
If you want to sell this coffee already toasted
and processed…

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:38.130
good luck!

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:42.090
You will have to pay a tariff of 7.5%.

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:43.440
Why?

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:47.080
Because Europe has factories that process
and toast coffee.

00:09:47.080 --> 00:09:50.010
And they don’t like competitors.

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:55.520
But I know what you’re thinking!

00:09:55.520 --> 00:09:59.530
Oh, that evil European Union is punishing
the third world!

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:02.990
Those Brussels bureaucrats want to destroy
free trade!

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:08.250
Well, I have bad news for you, guys… the
truth is EVERY country on this planet does

00:10:08.250 --> 00:10:09.860
the same thing.

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:14.880
In fact, when it comes to tariffs, Europe
is one of the most open regions.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:22.540
For instance, if this Argentinian farmer wanted
to sell his processed coffee in Japan, we

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:24.650
would pay a 20% tariff.

00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:30.890
If we wants to sell bananas, then we has to
pay between 40 and 50%, depending on the season.

00:10:30.890 --> 00:10:33.790
And what about America, the land of the free?

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:40.720
More or less, the same.

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:46.520
Almost no country is willing to leave its
farmers alone to compete in a fair competition.

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:49.220
Nonetheless, all of this can change.

00:10:49.220 --> 00:10:50.220
Why?

00:10:50.220 --> 00:10:54.010
Let’s have a look.

00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:58.440
BAD TIMES FOR PROTECTIONISM

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:03.340
Every year, Europe spends over 72 billion
dollars subsidizing its own farmers.

00:11:03.340 --> 00:11:07.880
But, as you can imagine, not all the country
members pay the same amount.

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:09.890
Just look at the chart.

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:12.260
Here you can see the main net contributors.

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:16.020
That is, the countries that pay more than
what they get.

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:17.950
And surprise!

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:18.950
Surprise!

00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:21.840
Who is the third biggest CAP net contributor?

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:30.100
Of course, that would be the United Kingdom,
the country that will no longer be a member

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:31.120
of the club.

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:35.310
As you can imagine, that leaves a hole of
more than 12 billion euros.

00:11:35.310 --> 00:11:39.480
This is why many are suggesting that other
countries have to start paying more to keep

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:46.090
the spending where it used to be.

00:11:46.090 --> 00:11:49.270
European Commission urges EU countries to
pay more into budget

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:51.720
Nonetheless, who wants to pay more?

00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:56.440
At a time where euroskeptic parties are stronger
than ever, no politician wants to come back

00:11:56.440 --> 00:12:00.590
to his own country saying “hey guys, take
out your wallet because now we gotta pay more

00:12:00.590 --> 00:12:03.240
for being in the EU”.

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:09.380
So… if you do the math, you realize that
there is just one option left.

00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:14.240
Brussels plans to cut its payments to Europe’s
biggest farms

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:18.620
Basically, Europe had to reduce the direct
aid given to the farmers.

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:22.800
And they have started with the wealthiest
ones, since they’re the ones who needed

00:12:22.800 --> 00:12:23.800
them the least.

00:12:23.800 --> 00:12:27.750
But the biggest surprise came with Emmanuel
Macron’s speech.

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:32.490
Everybody in Brussels might expect France
to come out as the biggest old school CAP

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:33.540
supporter, right?

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:35.840
But Macron has other priorities.

00:12:35.840 --> 00:12:39.270
And this means a green light to cut aid in
the future.

00:12:39.270 --> 00:12:44.190
Meanwhile, trade deals are in fashion.

00:12:44.190 --> 00:12:48.540
EU and Mexico agree new free trade pact

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:51.430
Yes, yes, yes… and we are not only talking
about Mexico.

00:12:51.430 --> 00:12:54.200
Look at this news…

00:12:54.200 --> 00:13:01.240
The European Union rushed to promote its preliminary
free-trade agreement with Japan by stressing

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:03.090
the benefits for EU farmers

00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:08.060
Yes, my dear viewers, slowly, the European
Union is reducing subsidies, reducing tariffs

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:10.560
and getting rid of production quotas.

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:13.300
And I know what you are thinking now…

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:14.300
Come on, Simon!

00:13:14.300 --> 00:13:18.630
Are you telling us that BREXIT is the only
reason for this change?

00:13:18.630 --> 00:13:19.630
Of course not!

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:21.840
In fact there are two other important reasons.

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:25.810
On one hand, European Agriculture is already
more productive than elsewhere.

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:30.850
In this video, we told you about the Netherlands
being one of the biggest agricultural powerhouses.

00:13:30.850 --> 00:13:33.860
In fact, they have what we can call the Food
Silicon Valley.

00:13:33.860 --> 00:13:35.520
But they are not the only ones!

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:37.770
Food exports can’t stop growing.

00:13:37.770 --> 00:13:42.340
In fact, things like Camembert Cheese or RIBERA
DEL DUERO wine can be found almost everywhere.

00:13:42.340 --> 00:13:44.360
And they come from Europe.

00:13:44.360 --> 00:13:48.810
In other words, Europe doesn’t need to protect
its own market anymore but, instead, sign

00:13:48.810 --> 00:13:50.410
lots of trade deals.

00:13:50.410 --> 00:13:52.840
And I know what you might be wondering now…

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:58.080
Will farmers be happy when they stop getting
all those subsidies from Brussels?

00:13:58.080 --> 00:13:59.540
Most likely…

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:00.540
no.

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:02.050
But, the question is…

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:03.880
Does it really matter?

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:07.910
Look, Peasants unions used to have a big political
grip.

00:14:07.910 --> 00:14:12.190
During the 60s, when De Gaulle was the French
President, France had millions of people working

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:13.260
in the primary sector.

00:14:13.260 --> 00:14:16.990
Now, they aren’t even 3% of the population.

00:14:16.990 --> 00:14:20.210
This means way less political sway and way
less votes.

00:14:20.210 --> 00:14:21.210
But not only that!

00:14:21.210 --> 00:14:27.730
Who do you think those European farmers vote
for?

00:14:27.730 --> 00:14:30.280
France's disillusioned farmers turn to Le
Pen

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:31.280
Exactly!

00:14:31.280 --> 00:14:34.760
Generally speaking, the biggest place for
euroskeptic parties to get their votes is

00:14:34.760 --> 00:14:37.750
the rural areas, where farmers live.

00:14:37.750 --> 00:14:41.940
So if you’re a European politician and you
want to make your voters happy… you wanna

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:47.660
pay more attention to the urban, Europeanist
voters, instead of the rural, euroskeptic

00:14:47.660 --> 00:14:48.660
ones.

00:14:48.660 --> 00:14:55.630
And your average city guy doesn’t even know
what the CAP is all about.

00:14:55.630 --> 00:14:59.160
That is why today’s question is…

00:14:59.160 --> 00:15:02.730
Does the CAP have any future whatsoever in
Europe?

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:07.260
Will we see greater liberalization of agriculture
in Europe and the rest of the world?

00:15:07.260 --> 00:15:10.230
Please, leave your answer in the comment section
below.

